# =============================================================================
# DevCouncil local workspace state
# (ignore everything under .devcouncil/ except the shared, checked-in files)
# =============================================================================
.devcouncil/*
!.devcouncil/config.yaml
!.devcouncil/secrets.env.example
# The codebase wiki is shared knowledge-as-code: checked in and maintained by
# `dev wiki update` (see .github/workflows/devcouncil-wiki-update.yml).
!.devcouncil/knowledge/
.devcouncil/knowledge/*
!.devcouncil/knowledge/okf/
.devcouncil/knowledge/okf/*
!.devcouncil/knowledge/okf/wiki/

# Generated benchmark outputs (benchmark source under benchmarks/ stays tracked)
# benchmarks/results/ is deliberately tracked: the correctness figures the
# README and CV cite (0.81 -> 0.94, 0 false negatives) are only checkable
# against the run that produced them.

# DevCouncil local state.
#
# A `.devcouncil/` at any depth is generated. The tools write their caches
# beside whatever root they were handed, so `dcgrep index` over `rust/dc-grep`
# leaves a `rust/dc-grep/.devcouncil/dcgrep/` holding an index cache — which
# git offered to commit, because every rule in the block above is anchored to
# the repository root and this negation was not anchored at all.
.devcouncil/
# Except the repository's own, which git has to descend into for the filtered
# negations above and the plugin-agent ones below to be reachable: git never
# looks inside a directory it has decided to ignore.
!/.devcouncil/

# =============================================================================
# Agent definitions: generated, and tracked so the check has something to read
# =============================================================================
# scripts/sync-agent-defs.mjs renders the Codex role and the plugin copy from
# .claude/agents/<name>.md. `npm run agents:check` compares the three, and on a
# clean clone it can only do that if all three are tracked: an untracked file
# reads as "missing", and a check that could not run must never report the same
# result as one that ran and passed.
#
# They were local state until three hand-maintained copies drifted apart — two of
# them telling agents to call `devcouncil_write_file`, `devcouncil_apply_patch`
# and `devcouncil_run_command`, which this host has not served since the Python
# host was retired.
#
# A pattern that excludes a directory stops git descending into it, so each level
# has to be re-included before the file itself can be. Same shape as the
# .devcouncil/knowledge rules above. Everything else under these directories —
# settings.local.json, commands/, worktrees/, the rest of the plugin — stays
# ignored.
!.claude/
.claude/*
!.claude/agents/
.claude/agents/*
!.claude/agents/devcouncil-*.md
!.codex/
.codex/*
!.codex/agents/
.codex/agents/*
!.codex/agents/devcouncil-*.toml
!.devcouncil/claude-plugin/
.devcouncil/claude-plugin/*
!.devcouncil/claude-plugin/devcouncil/
.devcouncil/claude-plugin/devcouncil/*
!.devcouncil/claude-plugin/devcouncil/agents/
.devcouncil/claude-plugin/devcouncil/agents/*
!.devcouncil/claude-plugin/devcouncil/agents/devcouncil-*.md

# The standalone spelling of the same directory. `devmap_extract::paths` picks
# `.devmap/` for any repository that does not already have a `.devcouncil/`,
# and writes the store, `repo_map.json`, `graph/code_graph.json` and the
# `cache/` memos into it — all generated, none of it checked in anywhere
# (`git ls-files .devmap` is empty). Only the store was ignored here, and only
# by accident, through the global `*.sqlite` rule.
.devmap/

# Environment, dependency, and cache directories
.env.local

